<DocsLayout
  title="Reactor recipes"
  description="Practical mappings you can build quickly without guessing which property to drive."
>
  <p>
    The fastest way to get better results with reactors is to bind them to a
    small number of high-impact properties. These recipes are good starting
    points, not rigid rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Bass-driven spectrum</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Create a reactor covering the low end of the spectrum.</li>
    <li>Add a <a href="/docs/displays">Bar Spectrum</a> display.</li>
    <li>Bind the reactor to height, scale, or amplitude.</li>
    <li>Raise smoothing until the motion feels punchy rather than jittery.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Glow on the beat</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Create a reactor tuned to kick or snare energy.</li>
    <li>Add a <a href="/docs/effects">Bloom</a> effect to the scene.</li>
    <li>Bind the reactor to intensity or threshold.</li>
    <li>Keep the range modest so the image breathes instead of washing out.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Floating title text</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Add a Text display for your artist name or track title.</li>
    <li>Create a midrange reactor.</li>
    <li>Bind the reactor to Y position, scale, or letter spacing.</li>
    <li>Use low strength and high smoothing for a soft drift.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Background motion without distraction</h2>
  <p>
    Put a background display such as Tunnel, Mesh Grid, or Noise in its own
    scene. Drive rotation, offset, or distortion with a broad full-range
    reactor. This gives the frame motion without competing with your main
    foreground element.
  </p>

  <h2>One reactor, many bindings</h2>
  <p>
    Reuse the same bass, mid, and high reactors across multiple displays and
    effects. Shared reactors make the composition feel coordinated and are much
    easier to tune than a different reactor for every property.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    If the motion feels chaotic, the fix is usually fewer bindings and more
    smoothing, not more reactors.
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
